  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The ongoing storm system winds down through the \nmorning as rain and snow come to an end for most by mid morning to \nearly afternoon while gusty winds stick around most of the day.  \n\nInfrared satellite imagery shows our much watched low pressure \ncenter spinning south of the region this morning directing gusty \nE/NE winds into southern New England. This, together with favorable \njet dynamics and an atmospheric column saturated through 500mb will \ncontinue to lead to widespread rain showers, flipping to some high \nelevation snow flakes over the next ~6 hours before the moisture and \nbetter lift is suppressed to the south. PWATs now as high as 0.75\" \naccording to mesoanalysis will drop closer to 0.5\" by 18z save for \nthe immediate south coast. Thus, the best chance of seeing showers \ncontinuing in the afternoon and evening will be along the south \ncoast of MA and RI though thick cloudcover remains entrenched \neverywhere. The tight pressure gradient sticks around so we'll see \nthose gusty N/NE winds continue throughout the day. The strongest \ngusts will be over Cape Cod and the islands. A Coastal Flood \nStatement remains in effect for northern facing shores of Cape Cod \nBay and Nantucket where said gusty winds combined with astronomical \ntides may lead to some minor coastal flooding/splashover in the \nhours directly around high tide this afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Lingering light rain showers and mist possible to \nclose out the weekend before high pressure marks the return of sunny \nskies and warming temperatures.\n\nA quick moving shortwave dropping south from Canada late Saturday \ninto Sunday will bring just enough enhanced moisture to allow for \nsome lingering shower chances mostly offshore, but can't rule out \nsome sprinkles and mist for the Cape and Islands. At the same time, \nhigh pressure begins to build over the Upper Midwest and push east, \ncompressing the pressure gradient and leading to prolonged breezy \nconditions across southern New England through a good portion of \nSunday as well. However, the strengthening high will mark the return \nof a dry period through most of next week. High temps slowly climb \nthrough the week... near 50 Sunday to near 60 by Wednesday. The high \npressure will also bring back several nights of radiational cooling \nwith clear skies and low winds, likely dropping low temperatures \ninto the 20s and low 30s through the week as well.",
